  ("WSM-ENVIRONMENT-DEEP-DROP-STACK-SAFETY" . (
    (priority . 7.5)
    (capabilities . (rust memory-safety correctness environment testing))
    (origin . my-lisp)
    (description . "crates/my-lisp/src/environment.rs already self-documents (comment at top of file) that dropping a deeply nested Environment recurses through Rc<RefCell<Frame>>'s default Drop and can stack-overflow -- the same failure class crates/my-lisp/src/value.rs already fixed for Value::Pair with a hand-rolled iterative Drop (worklist + unsafe ptr::read/ManuallyDrop) to avoid overflowing on a long list. Environment has not received the same treatment; the risk is documented but live, currently inactive only because typical programs don't nest let/currying deep enough to trigger it. Either give Environment the same iterative-Drop treatment as Value::Pair, or add a regression test that deliberately builds a very deep Environment chain and drops it, proving the current behavior (safe or not) rather than leaving it as an unverified comment. Do not change Environment's public API or lookup semantics.")
    (done . ()))
